      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(providers): add local Lume macOS leases",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- add a native `lume` SSH-lease provider for local Apple-silicon macOS VMs\n- clone one stopped golden image into one isolated VM per Crabbox lease\n- rotate SSH host keys on first clone boot, pin a per-lease host identity, and inject only a unique public key\n- serialize acquisition and enforce Apple's two-running-macOS-guest host limit\n- identity-fence the detached `lume run` owner, work around Lume 0.3.16 stop/readiness defects, and verify stop/delete before claim cleanup\n- add claim-safe release and multi-storage cleanup, trusted configuration, ARM64 target validation, docs, metadata, and live-smoke dispatch\n- add reusable macOS image hooks for first-boot identity and optional Cua Driver startup, plus noninteractive developer-tool setup improvements\n\n## Why\n\nCrabbox needs a local macOS backend that can reuse layered Lume images without baking credentials into them. Each session should have its own VM, filesystem, machine identity, SSH key, and host-key trust while still supporting normal Crabbox sync/run/release behavior.\n\nThis is separate from #678, which adds a cloud Linux CUA provider; this PR covers local macOS VMs on user-owned Apple-silicon infrastructure.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `go test -race ./internal/providers/lume ./internal/providers/all -count=1`\n- `go vet ./internal/providers/lume ./internal/providers/all`\n- full `go test ./internal/cli -count=1`\n- Windows ARM64 cross-compilation of the Lume provider\n- docs, provider-matrix, shell syntax, plist, and Node live-smoke checks\n- real Apple-silicon lifecycle with two concurrent leases from the same stopped golden image:\n  - distinct VM names, IPs, platform UUIDs, SSH keypairs, and ED25519 host-key fingerprints\n  - independent Crabbox commands at the same guest path\n  - Cua Accessibility, Screen Recording, and actual desktop capture passed in both VMs\n  - Tailscale remained installed but logged out with `NeedsLogin` in both VMs\n  - concurrent release left no VM, owner process, claim, key, known-hosts, private log, or temporary password-helper residue\n\n## Current boundaries\n\n- macOS/ARM64 only; Lume is not used for Windows or Linux guests\n- the prepared guest currently needs Lume's default bootstrap account/password; custom bootstrap passwords are not yet supported\n- desktop/webVNC bridging and Crabbox-managed Tailscale enrollment are intentionally not advertised yet\n- reusable image layers remain credential-free; credentials are injected only into disposable clones after boot\n",
        "branch": "agent/lume-macos-provider",
